from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-3585';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 2, weighted instances and Boolean languages; Section 6, especially Theorem 6.2; Section 7, missing nonnegative-weight case.'
refs += [dict(id='journal',title='Optimal Polynomial-Time Compression for Boolean Max CSP',authors='Bart M. P. Jansen; Michał Włodarczyk',year=2024,url='https://doi.org/10.1145/3624704',pdf_url='https://pure.tue.nl/ws/portalfiles/portal/347237483/3624704.pdf',locator='ACM Transactions on Computation Theory 16(1), article 4, March 2024. Definitions 2.1–2.3, Theorem 2.2, Proposition 4.3, Lemma 4.10, Theorem 6.2 and Section 7. The conclusion repeats the question.')]
notes=['Recovered the missing nonnegative-weight hypothesis and the exact exponential-base comparison from Theorem 6.2.','Defined fixed Boolean languages, repeated variable positions, integer weights, all polynomial weight bounds, and characteristic degree over the reals.','Made the NP-hard-language restriction explicit; otherwise all-zero/all-one tractable cases would defeat the intended classification.','Distinguished additive-variable reductions from linear-variable reductions and compression exponents from exact-time bases.','Read the March 2024 journal version, which still poses the question, and searched subsequent results.']
save(i,dict(title='Exact exponential-time equivalence for nonnegative Boolean Max-CSP',formal='Does the following extension of the source’s degree-based classification hold? For every fixed finite Boolean constraint language Γ that is neither 0-valid, 1-valid, nor 2-monotone, let d=deg(Γ). For every fixed real α>1, if Max CSP(Γ,N,c) has an exact deterministic algorithm running in αⁿn^{O(1)} time for every fixed constant c≥1, must Max d-CNF-SAT with nonnegative integer weights of total weight at most nᶜ also have an exact deterministic αⁿn^{O(1)}-time algorithm for every fixed c≥1? The same base α is required. Algorithms and polynomial factors may depend on Γ,α,c. This formulates the conclusion’s speedup question as extending Theorem 6.2 to all NP-hard nonnegative-weight languages; tractable languages are explicitly excluded.',definitions='A Boolean predicate f of arity r is a map {0,1}ʳ→{0,1}. A language Γ is a fixed finite set of such predicates, not part of an input instance. An instance Φ has variables x₁,…,xₙ and weighted applications (f,(i₁,…,iᵣ),w), where f∈Γ, each iⱼ∈[n] and w is a nonnegative integer. Indices may repeat. Arguments are variables themselves: constants and negated literals are available only if the corresponding predicate can already be expressed by a member of Γ with variable arguments. The value Φ(x) is the sum of weights whose predicate applications evaluate to 1. Max CSP(Γ,N,c) asks whether max_{x∈{0,1}ⁿ}Φ(x)≥t for an input integer t, under the promise that total weight is at most nᶜ. Weights, indices and t use binary encoding; duplicate applications can be combined and zero-weight applications discarded. Trivial thresholds outside the possible objective range are handled during input reading. Polynomial-time normalization is included; equivalently the runtime has an additional input-reading term for a redundantly listed representation. The normalized input has polynomial length for fixed Γ,c. The algorithm must answer every promised instance correctly, and n denotes variables, not clauses, total weight or bit length. For each f there is a unique multilinear real polynomial P_f agreeing with f on its Boolean cube. Its degree is the largest number of variables in a monomial with nonzero coefficient; deg(Γ)=max_{f∈Γ}deg(P_f). This is neither maximum predicate arity nor polynomial degree over F₂. Constants can be removed with a threshold adjustment. Disregarding constant predicates, a language is 0-valid if every predicate accepts its all-zero input, and 1-valid if every predicate accepts its all-one input. It is 2-monotone if each predicate has a DNF representation with at most two terms, one consisting only of positive variables and one only of negated variables. The excluded three cases are the source’s polynomial-time cases for nonnegative weights; the remaining cases are NP-hard and have d≥2. Max d-CNF-SAT is the same threshold maximization problem for disjunctions of at most d literals, with both signs of variables allowed, and nonnegative integer clause weights. Equivalently one may use the source’s d-ary clause language and repeated variables. All complexity statements are worst-case exact running times in an ordinary discrete computation model, up to polynomial factors. The premise is quantified over every fixed polynomial weight bound c; a result for only one small weight range does not supply that premise.',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove the displayed implication for every eligible fixed language Γ and every α>1, retaining the same exponential base and the all-c weight convention, or refute it by a language and a rigorously established failure of that implication. A theorem comparing only whether both problems are NP-hard, or comparing compression sizes or exponential rates after a constant-factor increase in variable count, does not establish the stated base-preserving classification.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Degree already classifies the signed-weight model',text='Theorem 6.2 places all degree-d signed-weight languages in the same exact exponential-base class as weighted Max d-CNF-SAT. The forward implication from Max d-CNF-SAT algorithms also covers its nonnegative-weight restrictions.',citation='journal'),dict(title='Two closure conditions settle special nonnegative cases',text='The same theorem covers nonnegative languages closed under predicate-output negation, f↦1−f, or under substitution of literals for arguments. An arbitrary NP-hard language need not have either closure.',citation='journal'),dict(title='Preserving variables is essential',text='The source’s additive reductions introduce only O(1) variables, so α^{n+O(1)} preserves α. Its general conversion to nonnegative weights can instead introduce a linear number of variables. That suffices for transferring a polynomial compression exponent but can change an exponential base.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The full journal article retains the gap',text='The March 2024 version repeats the nonnegative-weight speedup question after the exponential-time theorem. Its complete compression classification does not itself resolve the exact-time classification.',citation='journal')],why='Would show whether the algebraic degree of a Boolean constraint language determines its exact exponential-time complexity even when every constraint has the usual nonnegative weight.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2020',text='The ESA paper proves the signed-weight and closure-restricted exact-base classification, leaving general nonnegative languages open.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2024-03',text='The full TOCT article retains both Theorem 6.2 and the missing nonnegative-weight question.',citation='journal')],status='open',status_note='Checked the conference statement against the March 2024 version of record, including the exact-base quantifiers, all-c convention and tractability dichotomy. Subsequent searches through 12 September 2026 did not locate a general resolution. The displayed statement is the NP-hard-language extension of the source’s Theorem 6.2; this editorial interpretation of the shorter conclusion is explicit. Search is not exhaustive.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Recovered the conclusion as a same-base, all-polynomial-weight extension of Theorem 6.2, with the NP-hard-language restriction explicitly stated rather than importing tractable nonnegative languages.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A broad classification question linking Boolean algebraic degree to exact exponential-time algorithms under the standard sign restriction on weights. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['A Boolean Max-CSP instance asks for an assignment satisfying constraints of maximum total nonnegative weight.','Each fixed language has a degree defined by the real multilinear polynomials of its predicates.','The question asks whether an exact algorithm with exponential base α for any NP-hard degree-d language yields the same base for weighted Max d-CNF-SAT.','The known classification allows negative weights or imposes closure properties on the language.','The remaining issue is preserving the exponential base when removing the sign restriction; the 2024 journal article still asks it.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','journal'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
